__d(function(e,t,a,i,n,s,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:23,chapter_title:"What is Government?",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following best defines a government?",options:["A) A group of people who make money for the country","B) A system that manages a country \u2014 making laws, maintaining order, providing services, and representing citizens","C) A company that builds roads and bridges","D) A religious organisation that guides society"],correct_answer:"B) A system that manages a country \u2014 making laws, maintaining order, providing services, and representing citizens",explanation:"A government is the organised system through which a society manages its collective affairs \u2014 making and enforcing laws, providing public services (schools, hospitals, roads), maintaining security, and representing the nation in the world. Without government, complex societies with millions of people cannot function peacefully or equitably."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"India has how many levels (tiers) of government?",options:["A) One \u2014 only the Central Government","B) Two \u2014 Central and State","C) Three \u2014 Central, State, and Local","D) Four \u2014 Central, State, District, and Village"],correct_answer:"C) Three \u2014 Central, State, and Local",explanation:"India has three levels of government: (1) Central Government \u2014 governs the whole nation (defence, foreign affairs, railways, currency); (2) State Government \u2014 governs each state (police, agriculture, education, health); (3) Local Government \u2014 governs cities/towns (Municipal Corporations/Councils) and villages (Gram Panchayats). The 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments (1992) gave constitutional status to Local Government as India's third tier."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Universal adult franchise means:",options:["A) Only educated adults can vote","B) Only property-owning adults can vote","C) Every adult citizen \u2014 regardless of caste, religion, gender, or wealth \u2014 has the right to vote","D) Only men above 25 years can vote"],correct_answer:"C) Every adult citizen \u2014 regardless of caste, religion, gender, or wealth \u2014 has the right to vote",explanation:"Universal adult franchise means every adult (18+) Indian citizen has one vote in elections \u2014 regardless of caste, religion, sex, education, or economic status. This principle, enshrined in the Constitution, makes India a democracy: the richest industrialist and the poorest daily-wage worker have exactly equal say in choosing the government. India's first general election (1951\u201352) was the world's largest democratic exercise when introduced."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The building where the Karnataka Vidhan Sabha meets is:",options:["A) Raj Bhavan","B) Vidhana Soudha","C) Mysuru Palace","D) Town Hall, Bengaluru"],correct_answer:"B) Vidhana Soudha",explanation:"The Vidhana Soudha is the seat of Karnataka's State Legislature \u2014 where the Vidhan Sabha (224 MLAs) and Vidhan Parishad meet to make laws for Karnataka. It is located in Bengaluru and is one of India's most iconic government buildings, built in the Neo-Dravidian architectural style and completed in 1956. Raj Bhavan is the Governor's official residence."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Who is the actual head of the Karnataka State Government?",options:["A) The Governor of Karnataka","B) The President of India","C) The Chief Minister of Karnataka","D) The Speaker of the Vidhan Sabha"],correct_answer:"C) The Chief Minister of Karnataka",explanation:"In India's parliamentary system, the Chief Minister (CM) is the actual (functional) head of the State Government \u2014 the leader of the majority party in the Vidhan Sabha who runs the state executive with the Council of Ministers. The Governor is the constitutional (formal) head \u2014 a presidential appointee with mostly ceremonial functions. Real power lies with the elected Chief Minister, not the appointed Governor."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which house of India's Parliament is directly elected by the citizens?",options:["A) Rajya Sabha","B) Lok Sabha","C) Vidhan Sabha","D) Vidhan Parishad"],correct_answer:"B) Lok Sabha",explanation:"The Lok Sabha (House of the People) is directly elected by Indian citizens \u2014 voters in each of the 543 constituencies elect one Member of Parliament (MP). The Rajya Sabha (Council of States) is indirectly elected \u2014 State Legislative Assembly members (MLAs) vote to elect Rajya Sabha members. The Vidhan Sabha and Vidhan Parishad are state-level bodies, not part of Parliament."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the supreme law of India?",options:["A) The Indian Penal Code","B) The Parliament's most recent Act","C) The Constitution of India","D) The Prime Minister's orders"],correct_answer:"C) The Constitution of India",explanation:"India's Constitution is the supreme law \u2014 all other laws, orders, and government actions must conform to it. If Parliament passes a law that violates the Constitution, the Supreme Court can strike it down. Even the Prime Minister and all government agencies are bound by the Constitution. It establishes the structure of government, fundamental rights, and the limits of power. It came into effect on January 26, 1950 \u2014 celebrated as Republic Day."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The Election Commission of India is responsible for:",options:["A) Writing the Union Budget","B) Making laws for Parliament","C) Supervising free and fair elections to Parliament and State Legislatures","D) Appointing the Prime Minister"],correct_answer:"C) Supervising free and fair elections to Parliament and State Legislatures",explanation:"The Election Commission of India (ECI), established under Article 324 of the Constitution, is an independent constitutional body that conducts and supervises elections to the Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice President. It announces election schedules, enforces the Model Code of Conduct, registers parties, manages voter rolls, and declares results \u2014 all independently, without government interference."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Union List, State List, and Concurrent List in the Indian Constitution deal with:",options:["A) Classification of India's rivers","B) Division of law-making powers between Central and State governments","C) Listing of Fundamental Rights","D) Categories of Indian citizens"],correct_answer:"B) Division of law-making powers between Central and State governments",explanation:"The 7th Schedule of the Constitution contains three lists that divide legislative (law-making) powers: Union List \u2014 subjects on which only Parliament can make laws (defence, foreign affairs, banking, railways \u2014 100 subjects); State List \u2014 subjects on which only State Legislatures can make laws (police, agriculture, education, health \u2014 61 subjects); Concurrent List \u2014 subjects on which both Centre and States can make laws (forests, marriage, bankruptcy \u2014 52 subjects). This division is the basis of India's federal structure."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Karnataka's Vidhan Sabha has how many elected members?",options:["A) 100","B) 224","C) 543","D) 250"],correct_answer:"B) 224",explanation:"Karnataka's Vidhan Sabha (State Legislative Assembly) has 224 elected members \u2014 one MLA (Member of the Legislative Assembly) from each of the 224 assembly constituencies across Karnataka. MLAs are directly elected by voters in their constituency for a 5-year term. The party (or coalition) that wins majority of the 224 seats forms the state government, and its leader becomes the Chief Minister."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"India is described as a 'quasi-federal' state. What does this mean?",options:["A) India has no federal structure","B) India's federal structure gives the Centre more power than classic federations, especially during emergencies","C) States are more powerful than the Centre in India","D) India only pretends to be federal"],correct_answer:"B) India's federal structure gives the Centre more power than classic federations, especially during emergencies",explanation:"India is called 'quasi-federal' because while it divides powers between Centre and States (like a true federation), the Constitution gives the Centre significantly more power than states in classic federations like the USA. Features that tilt toward the Centre: (1) Governor is appointed by and represents the Centre; (2) During National Emergency, Centre can take over state subjects; (3) Centre can create or reorganise states; (4) Residual powers (not listed in any list) belong to Centre; (5) Centre controls most tax revenues. Hence 'quasi' (partially) federal."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which Constitutional Amendment gave constitutional status to Gram Panchayats and Urban Local Bodies?",options:["A) 42nd Amendment (1976)","B) 61st Amendment (1989)","C) 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992)","D) 86th Amendment (2002)"],correct_answer:"C) 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992)",explanation:"The 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj Institutions (Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti, Zila Panchayat) \u2014 creating the third tier of rural local government. The 74th Amendment did the same for Urban Local Bodies (Municipal Corporations, Municipal Councils, Nagar Panchayats). These amendments mandated elections every 5 years, reservation of seats for SC/ST and women, and devolution of powers/funds to local bodies. Karnataka was actually a pioneer \u2014 it established a 3-tier Panchayati Raj system under the Zilla Parishads, Taluk Panchayat Samithis, Mandal Panchayats and Nyaya Panchayats Act (1983)."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the role of the judiciary in a democracy?",options:["A) Making laws for Parliament","B) Running government departments","C) Interpreting laws, adjudicating disputes, and ensuring laws are constitutional and rights are protected","D) Conducting elections"],correct_answer:"C) Interpreting laws, adjudicating disputes, and ensuring laws are constitutional and rights are protected",explanation:"The judiciary (Supreme Court, High Courts, District Courts) is the third branch of government. It: (1) Interprets laws when their meaning is disputed; (2) Adjudicates disputes between citizens, or between citizens and government; (3) Reviews laws to ensure they conform to the Constitution (judicial review); (4) Protects Fundamental Rights \u2014 citizens can approach courts if rights are violated (Article 32). The judiciary is independent of the Executive and Legislature \u2014 judges cannot be easily removed \u2014 ensuring it can check the other branches without fear."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"How does a bill become a law in India?",options:["A) The Prime Minister announces it and it becomes law","B) It is introduced in Parliament, passed by both houses, and then receives Presidential assent","C) Citizens vote directly on each proposed law","D) The Supreme Court approves it"],correct_answer:"B) It is introduced in Parliament, passed by both houses, and then receives Presidential assent",explanation:"A Bill's journey to becoming law: (1) Introduced in Lok Sabha or Rajya Sabha; (2) Debated and voted on in that house; (3) Passed by majority; (4) Sent to the other house for debate and vote; (5) Passed by that house; (6) Sent to the President who gives assent (signs it); (7) Bill becomes an Act (law) and is published in the Official Gazette. The judiciary can later declare any Act unconstitutional. This multi-step process ensures laws are scrutinised carefully before enactment."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the role of the Rajya Sabha in India's Parliament?",options:["A) It passes the Union Budget independently","B) It represents India's states at the Centre and provides a second chamber of scrutiny for legislation","C) It directly elects the Prime Minister","D) It controls state governments"],correct_answer:"B) It represents India's states at the Centre and provides a second chamber of scrutiny for legislation",explanation:"The Rajya Sabha (Council of States) has two key roles: (1) Represents states \u2014 members are elected by State Legislative Assemblies, ensuring states have a voice in national law-making; (2) Second chamber of scrutiny \u2014 all bills (except Money Bills) must pass both houses, so the Rajya Sabha provides an additional check on hasty legislation. It is a permanent house (cannot be dissolved) \u2014 providing continuity. It is particularly important when one party has a majority in Lok Sabha but not Rajya Sabha \u2014 ensuring coalition and compromise in law-making."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the minimum age to vote in Indian elections?",options:["A) 21 years","B) 25 years","C) 18 years","D) 16 years"],correct_answer:"C) 18 years",explanation:"The minimum voting age in India is 18 years, reduced from 21 years by the 61st Constitutional Amendment (1988), which came into effect in 1989. This extension of franchise to 18\u201321-year-olds added crores of young voters to the electoral roll, reflecting the belief that young adults who are responsible enough to work, marry, and pay taxes should also have a voice in choosing their government. The voting age of 18 is the most common globally."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Karnataka government's KSRTC buses, government hospitals, and Rajiv Gandhi Housing Scheme are examples of:",options:["A) Private sector services","B) Public services provided by the State Government as part of its responsibility to citizens","C) Services provided by the Central Government only","D) Services provided by NGOs"],correct_answer:"B) Public services provided by the State Government as part of its responsibility to citizens",explanation:"KSRTC (Karnataka State Road Transport Corporation) buses, government hospitals (like Victoria Hospital, Bengaluru), and housing schemes are public services \u2014 provided by the Karnataka State Government as part of its constitutional responsibility to ensure citizens' welfare. State governments use tax revenues to provide services that the market cannot or does not provide equitably. These services reflect the State's List powers under the Constitution (transport, health, housing)."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"In India's parliamentary system, what happens when no party wins a clear majority in a Lok Sabha election?",options:["A) The election is held again immediately","B) The President rules the country directly","C) A coalition government is formed \u2014 two or more parties join together to achieve a majority and form a government","D) The army takes over temporarily"],correct_answer:"C) A coalition government is formed \u2014 two or more parties join together to achieve a majority and form a government",explanation:"When no single party wins 272+ seats (simple majority in 543-seat Lok Sabha), a hung Parliament results. In this case, parties form coalitions \u2014 alliances where multiple parties agree to govern together, sharing Cabinet positions. The leader of the largest coalition is invited by the President to form the government and prove majority within a set time. India has had several coalition governments: the United Front (1996\u201398), UPA I and II (2004\u201314), NDA coalitions. Coalition governments require negotiation and compromise \u2014 a feature of India's diverse multi-party democracy."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"The Constitution gives the Centre power to dismiss a State Government and impose President's Rule (Article 356). How does this affect India's federal character?",options:["A) It makes India a unitary state","B) It is a centralising provision that can override state autonomy, but is subject to judicial review and Parliamentary approval, making India 'quasi-federal'","C) It strengthens state governments","D) It has never been used"],correct_answer:"B) It is a centralising provision that can override state autonomy, but is subject to judicial review and Parliamentary approval, making India 'quasi-federal'",explanation:"Article 356 (President's Rule) allows the Centre to dismiss a state government if 'constitutional machinery has failed.' It has been used over 100 times, often controversially \u2014 sometimes to dismiss opposition state governments. The Supreme Court's Bommai judgment (1994) restricted misuse: President's Rule must be approved by Parliament within 2 months and is subject to judicial review. While this power tilts India toward centralism, the checks make it more balanced. The SC Bommai case specifically arose from Karnataka (1989), making this a historically significant Karnataka connection."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A citizen's Fundamental Right (Article 19 \u2014 freedom of speech) is restricted by the government on national security grounds. The citizen goes to court. What constitutional mechanism does the citizen use?",options:["A) Filing a complaint with the Election Commission","B) Filing a Writ Petition under Article 32 (Right to Constitutional Remedies) in the Supreme Court","C) Petitioning the Prime Minister directly","D) Filing a complaint with the Home Ministry"],correct_answer:"B) Filing a Writ Petition under Article 32 (Right to Constitutional Remedies) in the Supreme Court",explanation:"Article 32 \u2014 the Right to Constitutional Remedies \u2014 is called 'the heart and soul of the Constitution' by Ambedkar. It gives citizens the right to directly approach the Supreme Court if any Fundamental Right is violated. The citizen can file a writ petition (Habeas Corpus, Mandamus, Certiorari, Prohibition, or Quo Warranto) seeking the court's protection. The Supreme Court can order the government to restore the right or justify its restriction. This makes Fundamental Rights enforceable, not merely aspirational \u2014 the difference between a promise on paper and a right in practice."}]}},2749,[]);